They are made by people:

• <b>synthetic</b>: same composition and same structure as a natural gem (a synthetic ruby is a real red corundum);
• <b>with no counterpart in nature</b>: they do not exist naturally (YAG, cubic zirconia);
• <b>assembled gems</b>: several pieces glued together (doublets, triplets).

Acceptability is also a matter of <b>law</b>. Some materials cannot be sold freely, or not at all:

• <b>elephant ivory</b>: its international trade has been banned since 1989 (Washington Convention, CITES); the same goes for <b>rhinoceros horn</b>;
• <b>sea turtle shell</b>, <b>narwhal tusk</b>, some <b>corals</b>: very strictly regulated;
• <b>mammoth ivory</b>, which is fossil, is on the other hand allowed in many countries.

Rules change from one country to another: a seller must be able to prove the <b>origin</b> of the material, with a certificate. <b>Conflict diamonds</b> are also banned: the Kimberley process guarantees where diamonds come from.

A gemmologist must therefore be able to recognize these materials, and refuse to authenticate them without papers.
